{#
  Migration 0104 — THE ONE PERMITTED `UPDATE` IN THE OPERATIONAL ZONE.

  ARCHITECTURE.md §5.11 item 10 · §5.5 ("`retracted_by` is the single permitted UPDATE") ·
  §16 MI01, MI07 · docs/leads/kernel.md ruling D2.

  Everything evidentiary in this schema is append-only. `disposition` has exactly one exception,
  and the exception is narrow enough to state in one line: `retracted_by` may go from NULL to a
  value, once, and nothing else about the row may change.

  RETRACTION RE-OPENS THE GATE AND BUMPS THE EPOCH, and the second half is the interesting one.
  Re-opening is obvious — an obligation whose disposition was withdrawn is open again. Bumping
  the epoch means A RETRACTION AFTER ISSUE IS REFUSED BY THE EPOCH PIN, structurally, by the
  composite foreign key from the completion record with ON UPDATE RESTRICT. You cannot quietly
  withdraw the signature that let a permit merge; you must suspend the issued subject and fork a
  child whose gate is cleared afresh. That is git discipline — never rewrite a merged commit,
  open a revert branch — expressed as referential integrity.

  WHY THE COMPARISON IS EXHAUSTIVE AND NOT A LIST OF SIX COLUMNS.
  §5.11 compares six named columns: rationale, kind, signer_sub, check_id, virulence and
  signer_credential_id. Everything else on the row — the frozen competency snapshot, the
  projected requirement flags, `deliberation_seconds`, `prior_override_count`,
  `severity_snapshot`, the signature block, `expires_at` — was left mutable by omission. A guard
  that names six of forty columns is a guard whose completeness depends on nobody adding a
  forty-first, and this table gains columns every time the design learns something.

      (to_jsonb(NEW) - 'retracted_by') IS DISTINCT FROM (to_jsonb(OLD) - 'retracted_by')

  is the whole row minus the one permitted column, compared as data. It subsumes §5.11's six,
  it cannot fall behind a schema change, and it was MEASURED working on CockroachDB CCL v26.2.5
  (local single node, 2026-08-09) — `to_jsonb` over a trigger's row variable and JSONB key
  deletion by `-` are both accepted, and the UPDATE that changes only `retracted_by` passes while
  one that also changes another column raises.

  `IS DISTINCT FROM` rather than `<>`, because `<>` against a NULL yields NULL, and an `IF NULL`
  branch does not execute — so a change involving a nullable column would pass a `<>` guard
  silently. That is the same class of defect as comparing six columns instead of all of them,
  and it is the reason this file states the operator choice rather than assuming it.

  WHAT THIS FUNCTION IS NOT. It is not the append-only guard. `fn_refuse_mutation` (0107) refuses
  UPDATE and DELETE outright on the evidentiary tables; this one exists only because `disposition`
  is the single table where one UPDATE is legal. DELETE on `disposition` is not permitted by this
  function — it is a BEFORE UPDATE trigger and says nothing about DELETE, which is governed by
  the grant matrix and by `one_live_disposition` having nothing to point at afterwards. Naming
  DELETE here would imply this file defends it. It does not.

  PLATFORM: `(NEW).col` / `(OLD).col` for every COLUMN read; the whole-row references `NEW` and
  `OLD` passed to `to_jsonb()` are NOT parenthesised, and both spellings were measured.
#}
